[Event "Strategy: the outpost, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/OmJykCAg"]
[FEN "2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Today we think like a general. Plans, not tricks. First plan: the outpost. How do we get the knight to e6? [%csl Ge6][%cal Yc1d3,Gd3f4,Gf4e6] }
1. Nd3 { An outpost is a square no enemy pawn can ever attack. e6 is one: a knight there lives for ever, deep in his camp. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd3f4,Gf4e6] } (1. Ne2 { Also good. The same journey by another road: e2, f4, e6. [%cal Ge2f4] } 1... Rde8 2. Nf4 { The knight arrives all the same. [%csl Gf4] }) (1. g5 { A fine pawn storm, and it works too. But the knight had first claim on f4. } 1... g6 2. Nd3 { Now the knight sets off anyway. [%cal Gd3f4] }) (1. h5 { The pawn runs ahead, and he simply blocks it. } 1... h6 { Your pawn is stuck, and the knight has still not moved. [%csl Rh5] }) (1. e5 { A pawn shove with no follow-up. } 1... dxe5 { He simply takes it. A clean pawn down, and nothing to show for it. [%csl Re5] }) 1... Rde8 { He doubles on the e column. Keep walking: knight to f4. [%cal Yd3f4] } 2. Nf4 { From f4 the knight is one jump from e6. He cannot chase it: no pawn can ever reach f4 or e6. [%csl Gf4,Ge6][%cal Gf4e6] } (2. g5 { Also good. The knight lands on f4 next move anyway. } 2... fxg5 3. hxg5 Bd8 4. Nf4 { There it is. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Rd1 { Also good: the open column. But the knight comes first, the rooks after. } 2... g5 3. fxg6 hxg6 4. Nf4 { Now the knight joins in. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Kg2 { Tidy, and it keeps everything. But it is a waiting move. } 2... Qd7 3. Nf4 { The knight still had to go. [%csl Gf4] }) 2... Bd8 { He shuffles the bishop. Now the last jump. [%cal Yf4e6] } 3. Ne6 { Home! The knight sits on e6 for ever. Only a rook can remove it, and a rook for a knight is a price he must pay. [%csl Ge6] } (3. Rd1 { Also good: take the open column first. But finish the plan, the knight was one jump away. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd5 Kg8 { Still winning, and e6 is still empty. [%csl Re6] }) (3. g5 { Also good, a pawn storm. The knight goes to d5 instead. } 3... fxg5 4. hxg5 Qa8 5. Nd5 { Another outpost, and also winning. [%csl Gd5] }) (3. Rg2 { A rook move. Fine, but the knight was ready. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd2 Bc7 { Still winning. The outpost is still empty. [%csl Re6] }) (3. Kg2 { A waiting move. Also fine. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd1 Bc7 { Still winning, but the knight has not arrived. }) (3. Qd3 { The queen eyes d6. Also fine. } 3... Qd7 4. h5 h6 { Still winning: his bishop on d8 has one square to go and nowhere after it, while your knight can still walk into e6 or d5 whenever you like. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, Fischer's knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
