[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/LGsz0l4h"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1 w k - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1_w_k_-_0_1"]

{ Same idea again: the outpost. Look at d5 — your pawns guard it, and no black pawn can ever reach it. Get a knight there. [%csl Gd5] }
1. Nb6 { He cannot take back: grabbing the knight walks into mate on d7. The knight is safe — next stop d5. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb6d5,Rd3d7] } (1. Ne1 { Also on its way to d5, via d3. But it is the slow road. } 1... Be7 2. Qe2 O-O 3. Nd3 { You will get there, but he has castled and tidied up. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. h4 { You poke his knight on g6, but the plan was your own knight, not his. } 1... Be7 2. h5 Rd8 { He develops and grabs the open column. Your knight is still stuck on the rim. [%csl Ra4] }) (1. Nxc5 { Grabbing a pawn, but it costs a piece. } 1... Bxc5 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 { The bishops trade off even. What is left: a knight lost for one pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. Qc2 { A quiet move, and the whole idea evaporates. } 1... Bd6 2. Rd2 Be7 { Nothing happened. The knight on a4 is still doing nothing. [%csl Ra4] }) 1... Rd8 { He challenges the d column. Do not stop now. } 2. Nd5 { Home. The knight sits on d5 for good, and every black piece has to live around it. [%csl Gd5] } (2. Qxd8+ { Also good. Trade the rooks first, then the knight lands on d5 anyway. } 2... Qxd8 3. Rxd8+ Kxd8 4. Nd5 { The same knight, the same square. [%csl Gd5] }) (2. Qc2 { Careful: the knight on b6 is hanging. } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { A whole knight gone. Your queen sits on c2, three squares from the knight it needed to defend. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qe2 { The queen steps aside and forgets the knight. } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { A whole knight gone. Your queen went to e2 and simply lost sight of b6. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qd7+ { A check that gives away the queen. } 2... Rxd7 3. Nxd7 { A queen for a rook. Lost. [%csl Rd7] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, a home for the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/8QEYZaHM"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 11"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R_w_KQkq_-_3_11"]

{ Your knight on f5 is a monster — an outpost. He wants g4 to kick it away. How do you stop that for good? }
11. O-O { He castles, and g4 is coming next. How do you keep the knight on f5? [%csl Rg4] } 11... h5 { The pawn watches g4: if he ever pushes it, you just capture, and the knight on f5 never has to move. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh5g4][%tail] } (11... bxc3 { You open the b column, but for whom? } 12. bxc3 h5 13. Rb1 { His rook is on b1 first. Playable, but you helped him. [%csl Rb1] }) (11... c4 { Too early. Now g4 comes with a hit. } 12. g4 cxd3 13. Qxd3 { The knight must move and his pawns roll. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (11... g6 { You guard f5 from the wrong side. } 12. g4 Ng7 { Kicked back to g7, the saddest square. [%csl Rg7] }) (11... Be7 { Development is nice, but the knight goes first. } 12. g4 Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 { The knight is traded off. The outpost is gone. [%csl Rf5] }) (11... Nh4 { Straight at his king, and straight into two different captures. } 12. Nxh4 { Gone for nothing — the pawn on g3 could have taken it too. [%cal Rg3h4] }) 12. a3 12... a5 13. h3 13... Be7 { The bishop steps to e7 and you are ready to castle. His pawns on f4, g3 and h3 still cannot budge the knight on f5 — push g4 and you simply capture, and the knight never has to move an inch. [%csl Gf5] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep the outpost"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/4Ts0CCtQ"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/3b1ppp/3p4/2nPp3/1p2N3/6P1/1P2PPBP/1QR2RK1 b - - 0 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/3b1ppp/3p4/2nPp3/1p2N3/6P1/1P2PPBP/1QR2RK1_b_-_-_0_22"]

{ The right trade. How do you keep your strong knight at c5? [%csl Gc5] }
22... Bf5 { Bishop to f5: it lines up on the knight, ready to trade itself for it. His bishop on g2 stares at a wall. [%cal Gf5e4][%csl Rg2] } (22... Nb3 { Also strong, a sharp trick that wins the exchange. But today the lesson is the trade. } 23. Nxd6 Qb8 24. Ne4 Nxc1 25. Qxc1 { A rook for a knight and a pawn. Good for you, and not our plan. }) (22... Nxe4 { You traded the wrong knight. Yours was the hero. } 23. Qxe4 Qb6 24. Rc4 { His queen sits in the middle and your b pawn is a target. Equal. [%csl Rb4] }) (22... Nb7 { The knight retreats from its best square. } 23. Rc4 f5 24. Rxb4 { The b pawn is gone. [%csl Rb4] }) (22... Qb6 { You guard the b-pawn, but forget the real point: it was the knight on c5 that needed keeping. } 23. Nxc5 dxc5 24. Rfd1 { He simply trades it off. His bishop lives, and your pawns are a mess. [%csl Rc5,Rb4] }) 23. Rc4 { He swings the rook to c4, hitting the b pawn. [%csl Rb4] } 23... b3 { Safe on b3, and it fixes his b2 pawn for ever. That pawn is the target. [%csl Rb2] } (23... Rb8 { You guard it from behind, but the pawn on b4 is still a target. } 24. b3 Rb5 25. Qa1 { The pawn is stuck and his rook sits on c4. Only a little better for you. [%csl Rb4] }) (23... Bxe4 { The trade, but one move early. } 24. Bxe4 g6 25. Rxb4 { The b pawn went for nothing, and the trade you wanted is already done anyway. [%csl Rb4] }) (23... g6 { A useful move, but the b pawn is hanging. } 24. Rxb4 Bxe4 25. Bxe4 { A pawn gone. Equal: your knight on c5 is worth at least as much as his extra pawn on d5. }) 24. Qc1 { He steps his queen off the pin, unpinning the knight. } 24... Bxe4 { Now the trade. His good piece is gone, and the knight on c5 stays for ever. [%csl Gc5] } (24... Na6 { The knight walks away from c5 and the whole plan with it. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { Back where it started, and his knight escaped. Equal. [%csl Rd2] }) (24... Nxe4 { Wrong knight again. } 25. Bxe4 Bxe4 26. Rxe4 { Everything traded and his rook is active. Equal. }) (24... Na4 { The knight goes hunting on the side. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { And walks back. Nothing gained. }) 25. Bxe4 { He recaptures on e4 with the bishop. [%csl Re4] } 25... f5 { The pawn shoos the bishop away. Knight against bad bishop, and the b2 pawn is a target for ever. [%csl Gc5,Rb2][%cal Gf5e4] } (25... Ra4 { Also good: trade rooks and keep squeezing. } 26. Rxa4 Nxa4 27. Kg2 { Still clearly better for you. [%csl Ga4] }) (25... Qd7 { Fine too, but why not chase the bishop first? } 26. Bb1 Qb5 { Still better for you, but the bishop got out. }) (25... g6 { Also fine. The bishop can still sit on e4 though. } 26. Bd3 Qf6 { Still better, a little slower. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, the stonewall bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/lPzDl6I7"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 0 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_0_7"]

{ Another right trade: his dark bishop on d6 guards every hole in his camp. Which piece should challenge it? [%csl Rd6,Ge5,Gc5] }
7... O-O { He castles. Which piece holds his whole position together? [%csl Rd6] } 8. Ba3 { Bishop to a3: trade off his dark square defender. e5, c5 and f4 will be yours. [%cal Ga3d6][%csl Ge5,Gc5] } (8. Nc3 { Development, but his bishop on d6 lives on. } 8... Qe7 9. e3 Ne4 { He is solid and the dark squares are still his. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Bb2 { The bishop hides behind its own pawn and stares at d4. } 8... Nbd7 9. Qc2 Ne4 { A normal game. The trade was the point. [%csl Rb2] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but the bishop on d6 will chase it or trade it. } 8... Nbd7 9. Nd3 Qe7 { Nothing gained: your knight sits on d3 early, but his bishop on d6 is still there, unchallenged. }) (8. e3 { Fine, and Ba3 can still come next move. } 8... Bd7 9. Ba3 Bxa3 10. Nxa3 { The same trade, one move later. Also good. [%csl Ga3] }) 8... Bxa3 { He takes. Take back with the knight, and it is on its way to d3. } 9. Nxa3 { The knight will come round: c2, e1, d3. [%cal Ga3c2,Gc2e1,Ge1d3] } 9... Qe7 { He eyes your knight. Where does it go? } 10. Nc2 { Round to c2 first. One more hop to e1, then d3 — deep in your own camp, where nothing black owns can ever touch it. [%cal Gc2e1,Ge1d3][%csl Gd3][%tail] } (10. Qc1 { Also good: the queen takes over the a3 to f8 diagonal. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qb2 { The same idea with the queen. Fine. [%cal Gb2f6] }) (10. Nb1 { Back home. It gets to d2, but it is the slow road. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qc1 e5 { He frees himself while you regroup. }) (10. c5 { The pawn goes forward and becomes a target. } 10... b6 11. b4 a5 { He undermines it at once. Equal. [%csl Rc5] }) (10. b4 { A free pawn for him. } 10... Qxb4 { Simply gone: his queen scoops up the pawn and there is no way to win it back. [%csl Rb4] }) 10... Nbd7 11. Rc1 11... b6 12. Nce1 12... Ba6 13. Nd3 { Home on d3, tucked behind your own c and d pawns. No black pawn can ever get near it, and the knight sits there for the rest of the game. [%csl Gd3] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep his weaknesses"]
[Result "1-0"]
