[FEN "r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_1"]

{ Another right trade: his e pawns are doubled, and they hurt more as pieces come off. Which trade? }
1. Bh5 { Bishop to h5: offer the trade. His bishop on g6 is his best piece, and it holds the h7 pawn. [%cal Gh5g6] } (1. h4 { Useful later, but not yet: ignoring the centre lets him strike first. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 Bf7 { He trades for the bishop pair, and your kingside pawns are wrecked too. Only a little better. [%csl Re3] }) (1. O-O { Safe, but slow. } 1... Bf5 2. Bg5 g6 { He tidies up. A little better for you, no more. }) (1. Bg5 { Not the trade you want — this eyes the long diagonal instead. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { The trade comes anyway, but his queen got a head start. About equal. }) (1. Nc4 { The knight eyes e5, and he just guards it. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { Nothing gained: the same trade happens two moves later, and the knight on c4 was not needed for it. }) (1. Bf3 { It attacks the knight, but he has a counter-shot of his own. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 e6 { He wins a bishop for a knight, and your pawns are wrecked. He is better now. [%csl Re3] }) 1... e6 { He keeps the bishop. Trade anyway. } 2. Bxg6+ { Take it. His best piece is gone, and his pawns are fixed for good. [%csl Rg6] } (2. Bf3 { You back off, and your bishop on e3 hangs. } 2... Nxe3 3. fxe3 Qh4+ { A wrecked centre and a king in the open. Lost. [%csl Re3] }) (2. Ne4 { A jump into the middle. It works, but the trade was simpler. } 2... Qh4 3. Bxg6+ hxg6 { You traded anyway. Also fine. }) (2. O-O { Castle first, and he takes the bishop himself. } 2... Qf6 3. Bxg6+ Qxg6 { His queen lands in the centre, delighted to recapture. Only a little better. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
