[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/6x6tlAtk"]
[FEN "r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1 b - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1_b_-_-_0_21"]

{ New plan: improve your worst piece. Look at the knight on e8: it does nothing. Give it a road. [%csl Re8][%cal Yg7g6,Ge8g7,Gg7f5] }
21... g6 { The pawn opens g7 for the knight. From g7 it reaches f5, its dream square. [%cal Ge8g7,Gg7f5][%csl Gf5] } (21... Be7 { The bishop steps back, and the knight still sleeps. } 22. Bf1 Rc8 23. h3 Nf6 { It got out by another road, slower. Still a bit worse. [%csl Re8] }) (21... Kh8 { A waiting move. The knight is still asleep. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Be5 { He improves too. Slightly worse. }) (21... Rd8 { You trade rooks and the knight still has no square. } 22. Bf1 Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Be7 { Nothing solved. A little worse. }) (21... Rc8 { The rook moves, the knight waits. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Rd4 { He is a little better. Improve the worst piece first. }) 22. Bg5 { He fights for the dark squares and hits your bishop. Keep the plan. [%cal Yf6g7] } 22... Bg7 { The bishop steps to g7, and the knight's road is clear: e8 to f6 is open. [%cal Ge8f6] } (22... Bh8 { Also good. The bishop tucks away and g7 stays free for the knight. } 23. Qf4 Bg7 24. Qh4 Nc7 { The same plan, a move later. }) (22... Bxg5 { You give up the bishop pair, but the knight still gets out. Also fine. } 23. Qxg5 Ng7 24. Bd3 Rfd8 { The knight goes to f5 next. [%cal Gg7f5] }) (22... Rc8 { A rook move while your bishop is attacked. } 23. Bd3 Bxg5 24. Qxg5 Ng7 { Fine, but a little slower. }) (22... h5 { You weaken the king side for nothing. } 23. Bd3 Rd8 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 { The bishop pair is gone and your king is drafty. Worse. [%csl Rh5] }) 23. Bf1 { He tucks his bishop away. Now the knight jumps. [%cal Ye8f6] } 23... Nf6 { The knight lives. From f6 it reaches d5 and e4, and it is your best piece now. [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf6d5,Gf6e4] } (23... h6 { Also good: kick the bishop first, the knight jumps next. } 24. Bf4 Nf6 { And it comes out anyway. [%csl Gf6] }) (23... h5 { Fine too. But the knight was ready to jump now. } 24. h3 Nf6 { Same knight, one move later. }) (23... Rc8 { The rook moves, and the bishop on g5 gets to h6. } 24. Qh4 Nc7 25. Bh6 { He trades your good bishop. A little worse. [%csl Rh6] }) (23... Bf6 { You offer the trade you were avoiding. } 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 25. Qe5 { The bishop pair is gone and his queen sits in the middle. Worse. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, the sleeping rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/r6CnOaY7"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_18"]

{ Same idea again: a piece not pulling its weight. Your rook on a8 sleeps — find it a road to the king side. }
18. Qb5 { His queen eyes b7 and d5. Which piece needs a job? [%csl Ra8] } 18... a5 { The a pawn steps out and the rook has a door: a6, then g6. [%cal Ga8a6,Ga6g6] } (18... a6 { Also good: the same door, one step smaller. } 19. Qb1 a5 20. Ba3 Rf7 { The rook comes out next. Still winning. [%csl Ga8] }) (18... Rf7 { The other rook lifts, but the a8 rook still sleeps. } 19. a5 Bg4 20. Ba3 { His bishop is out and a5 shuts your door. Still better, but less. [%csl Ra5] }) (18... Bg4 { The bishop comes out, but where is the a rook going? } 19. Ba3 Rf7 20. Qc5 { His pieces wake up. Only a little better. }) (18... Rf6 { The f rook wanders, and the a rook stays asleep. } 19. Ba3 Kh7 20. Qb1 { His bishop is out and you are only a bit better. [%csl Ra8] }) (18... Qg5 { The queen attacks alone. } 19. Kh1 Qh5 20. Ba3 { Nobody helps her. Only a little better. }) 19. Ra2 { He tucks the rook away. Now bring yours out. } 19... Ra6 { Out along the sixth, off the back rank at last. [%cal Ga6g6][%csl Rg2][%tail] } (19... Rf7 { Also good. The f rook first, the a rook next. } 20. Ba3 Ra6 21. Qb1 Qg5 { Both rooks are coming. Winning. [%cal Ga6g6] }) (19... Rf5 { The rook goes up the f column instead. Fine, but the a rook still waits. } 20. f3 exf3 21. Rxf3 Rxf3 22. gxf3 { The king is open, but you traded your attacker. Still better. }) (19... Rf3 { Bold, and it gets stuck out there. } 20. Kh1 Rf7 21. f4 { You had to go back. Still better, but the rook lost time. [%csl Rf3] }) (19... Rf6 { Sideways, and it blocks the sixth rank for the other rook. } 20. Qb1 Raa6 21. Ba3 { Two rooks in each other's way. Still better. }) 20. Ba3 20... Rf5 { The other rook lifts to f5. Both rooks are in the game now, and his bishop still has nothing to bite on. [%csl Gf5,Ga6] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, do nothing well"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/uzv8EH7h"]
[FEN "r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1 b - - 0 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1_b_-_-_0_22"]

{ Same idea, another piece: your knight on f3. He is tangled up — regroup quietly, and do not let him out. }
22... Kxg7 { He takes the bishop back. His pieces are in a knot. What now? [%csl Rc5,Rd7] } 23. Nd2 { A quiet knight move: it eyes e4 and c4, and gives him nothing to hit. He stays tangled. [%cal Gd2e4,Gd2c4] } (23. c4 { You rush, and untangle him for free. } 23... dxc4 24. Ne5 Nxe5 25. Qxe5+ { Playable, but his knots are gone. [%csl Gc5] }) (23. Rb4 { The rook climbs to the fourth row, with nothing there to attack. } 23... Qc7 24. Qe1 Nf6 { It did nothing there. Still better. }) (23. Ne5 { You trade your good knight. } 23... Nxe5 24. Qxe5+ Kg8 { Trades help the tangled side. Still better, but less. }) (23. h4 { A pawn move on the wrong side. } 23... Rb8 24. h5 Qc7 { He unwinds. Still better, but less. }) 23... Nf6 { He brings the knight back. Now the pawn. } 24. d4 { The pawn hits the knight on c5. It has nowhere good to go. [%cal Gd4c5][%csl Rc5] } (24. c4 { Also good, but the knight on c5 gets to stay. } 24... h5 25. d4 Nce4 { The same idea, a move later. Still better. }) (24. h4 { Slow: a kingside pawn push does nothing about the knight stuck on c5. } 24... Rd8 25. Qe5 h5 { He is organised now. A little better only. }) (24. Qe3 { The queen eyes c5, and he pushes with tempo. } 24... Rb8 25. c4 d4 { His pawn hits your queen. Less. }) 24... Nce4 { The knight jumps to e4. Trade it off. } 25. Bxe4 { Take with the bishop first. [%cal Gd2e4] } (25. Nxe4 { Also good, the same trade in the other order. } 25... dxe4 26. Bxe4 Nxe4 27. Qxe4 { A pawn up. [%csl Ge4] }) (25. Rba1 { You ignore the knight, and it forks. } 25... Nc3 26. Qe5 Nxa2 { A rook gone. [%csl Ra2] }) (25. c4 { A pawn move while a knight sits on e4. } 25... Nc3 26. Qd3 Nxa2 { The fork again. A rook gone. }) 25... dxe4 { He takes back. Now collect. } 26. Nxe4 { A pawn up, and his knot has turned into a lost pawn. [%csl Ge4] } (26. Rab2 { The rook steps away, and he gets a hit. } 26... Qc3 27. Nxe4 Nxe4 28. Qxe4 Qxa5 { You got a pawn, he got the a pawn. Only a little better. }) (26. Rb4 { Slow: the rook takes a detour while the pawn on e4 is still there for the taking. } 26... Qc6 27. c4 Red8 { He holds everything. Only a little better. }) (26. Nc4 { The knight goes the wrong way. } 26... Nd5 27. Rb3 Qc7 { His knight sits on d5. Only a little better. }) (26. Qxe4 { The queen steps onto a knight's square. } 26... Nxe4 { Gone: his knight takes yours right back on e4. [%csl Re4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, stop his plan"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/ckZZnXY6"]
[FEN "r1b2bk1/1pq3pp/p2pp3/4n1PB/3QP3/2N1B3/PPP4P/5RK1 w - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2bk1/1pq3pp/p2pp3/4n1PB/3QP3/2N1B3/PPP4P/5RK1_w_-_-_0_18"]

{ His plan is bishop to d7, then c6, and he is fine. Your knight on c3 is doing nothing. Fix both at once. }
18. Nd5 { Knight to d5: if the pawn takes, your queen retakes with check. Meanwhile his bishop to d7 walks into Nb6. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5b6] } (18. Bf4 { A natural move, and he plays the move you had to stop. } 18... Bd7 19. Qf2 Be8 { His bishop is out. Equal. [%csl Gd7] }) (18. Na4 { The knight goes to the rim, and a pawn kicks it. } 18... b5 19. Nb6 Rb8 20. Nxc8 { Traded off. Equal. }) (18. h4 { A pawn move, and the bishop comes out. } 18... Bd7 19. Qd2 Be8 { His plan worked. Slightly worse: a rook pawn push bought him the tempo he needed for Bd7. [%csl Gd7] }) (18. Qd2 { The queen steps back, and the bishop comes out. } 18... Bd7 19. Bd4 g6 { His plan worked. Slightly worse: the queen retreat let his bishop out just the same. }) 18... Qc6 { He steps aside and hits the knight. Where does it go? } 19. Nb6 { To b6. The rook must move, and the bishop on c8 still has not moved. [%csl Gb6,Rc8][%cal Gb6c8] } (19. Nb4 { Also playable, but the queen comes back and you are dancing. } 19... Qc7 20. Nd5 Qc6 { Back where you were. }) (19. Qb6 { You offer the queens, and he is happy. } 19... Bd7 20. Qxc6 Bxc6 { His bishop is out and the queens are gone. Slightly worse. [%csl Gc6] }) (19. Rxf8+ { You trade the rook and lose the thread. } 19... Kxf8 20. Nb6 Rb8 { Still the knight on b6, but you gave up the f column. Slightly worse. }) (19. Nc3 { Back home, and his bishop comes out. } 19... Bd7 20. Be2 b5 { His plan worked. Worse. [%csl Gd7] }) 1-0


