[FEN "2b1k3/1p3pn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P2PB1/1P4PP/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2b1k3/1p3pn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P2PB1/1P4PP/4K3_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ New plan: the weak colour. All his pawns stand on light squares, so the dark squares are yours. Bishop to e5. [%csl Ge5,Gd6,Gf6][%cal Yg3e5] }
1. Be5 { The bishop takes the best dark square on the board. The knight on g7 is stuck behind it. [%csl Ge5,Rg7] } (1. Kf2 { The king first. But he frees himself: pawn takes e4 and the knight comes to f5. } 1... dxe4 2. Be5 Nf5 { The knight escaped. Still better, but less. [%csl Gf5] }) (1. Ke2 { Slow: the king moves while the bishop still has not even reached a dark square. } 1... Ke7 2. Bh4+ f6 { He blocks with a pawn on a dark square. Still better, but less. }) (1. Bd6 { The wrong dark square: the king walks past it. } 1... Kd8 2. Be5 Ne8 { You go to e5 anyway, a move later. Less. }) (1. exd5 { You open the position for his pieces. } 1... exd5 2. Bh4 Ne6 { His knight wakes up. Equal. [%csl Ge6] }) (1. Nxb7 { Grabbing the pawn looks tempting. } 1... Bxb7 { The bishop simply takes the knight. [%cal Rc8b7] }) 1... Kf8 { He steps away from the diagonal. Now the knight: to a4, then b6. [%cal Yc5a4,Ga4b6] } 2. Na4 { The knight reroutes to b6, another dark square, and hits the bishop and the pawns. [%cal Ga4b6][%csl Gb6] } (2. Bxg7+ { Also good, but you traded your monster for his worst piece. } 2... Kxg7 3. Na4 Bd7 { Still winning. The bishop was prettier. }) (2. Bd6+ { Also fine: a different dark square, same idea. The king steps back, and the knight comes next. } 2... Kg8 3. Na4 Bd7 { Same plan, a move later. }) (2. Bf6 { You chase the knight and it walks away. } 2... Ne8 3. e5 Nc7 { The knight is out. Still better, but less. }) (2. Kd2 { Slow: shuffling the king wastes the move the knight needed to reroute. } 2... Ne8 3. Na4 Ke7 { The knight escaped. Less. }) 2... Bd7 { The bishop guards b5 and c6. Keep coming. [%cal Ya4b6] } 3. Nb6 { On b6 the knight hits the bishop. Every move is on a dark square. [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb6d7] } (3. Nc5 { Back to c5, and you are going in circles. } 3... Bc8 { Back where you started. [%csl Rc5] }) (3. Bxg7+ { Also good, but the bishop was the monster. } 3... Kxg7 4. e5 h4 { Still winning, but slower. }) (3. Bd6+ { Also fine: check first, though the knight still gets there next move. } 3... Kg8 4. Nc5 Bc8 { You are shuffling. Still winning. }) 3... Be8 { The bishop hides on e8. Now lock the door. [%cal Ye5f6] } 4. Bf6 { Bishop to f6. Every black pawn is stuck on a light square, and your king is about to march right through the dark ones. [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge1f2,Gf2e3][%tail] } (4. Nc8 { The knight goes hunting and gets nothing. } 4... b5 5. Bf6 dxe4 { You had to come back to f6 anyway. Still winning. [%csl Rc8] }) (4. Na4 { Back again: the knight returns to where it started, and gets nothing for the lost tempo. } 4... dxe4 5. fxe4 f5 { He gets some air. Still winning, but you wasted a move. }) (4. Kf2 { The king first. } 4... f5 5. Na4 g5 { His pawns get moving. Still better, but less. }) 4... Kg8 5. Kf2 5... Kh7 6. Ke3 6... g5 7. Bxg5 { The bishop just takes it: a whole pawn, and his knight still has no dark square to land on. [%csl Gg5] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, aim the queen"]
