[FEN "2r3k1/1p1qbppp/p1r1pn2/2Pp4/PP1B4/4P3/4BPPP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 2 20"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/1p1qbppp/p1r1pn2/2Pp4/PP1B4/4P3/4BPPP/2RQ1RK1_b_-_-_2_20"]

{ Another weak colour: he has no light bishop left, so those squares are yours. But a rook blocks your pawns first. }
20... R6c7 { The rook steps back. He is passive. Which piece can gain a move on it? [%csl Rc7] } 21. Be5 { Bishop to e5, hitting the rook. A move gained, and it clears d4 for the queen. [%cal Ge5c7][%csl Gd4] } (21. Qb3 { The queen guards b5, but he plays a5 himself and everything freezes. } 21... a5 22. Be5 axb4 23. Bxc7 Qxc7 { Still better, but the pawns are stuck. [%csl Rb4] }) (21. a5 { Also good, but the rook is not chased. } 21... Ne8 22. Be5 Bd6 { A little slower. Still better. }) (21. Qc2 { A quiet move, and he gets a5 in. } 21... a5 22. Bb5 Qd8 { Your pawns are frozen. Equal. [%csl Ra5] }) (21. b5 { Too soon: the pawn break comes before the bishop even gets to e5. } 21... axb5 22. Be5 Rxc5 { The c pawn is gone. Worse. [%csl Rc5] }) (21. Bxf6 { You trade your good bishop for the knight. } 21... Bxf6 22. a5 g6 { His bishop is happy now. Only a little better. [%csl Gf6] }) (21. Rb1 { Slow, and he plays a5. } 21... a5 22. Be5 axb4 { Only a little better: the rook move gained nothing, and now his a pawn is gone too. }) 21... Rc6 { The rook is back on c6. Now the light squares. } 22. a5 { Pawn to a5: b6 is yours, his bishop is dead on the dark squares, and b7 can never move. [%csl Gb6,Rb7][%cal Gb4b5] } (22. h3 { Fine, but a5 was ready. } 22... Ne4 23. Bd3 Bf6 { Still better: a5 was ready to play at once, and h3 only delayed it. }) (22. Qd4 { The queen comes to d4 first. Also fine. } 22... Qd8 23. a5 Qf8 { The same plan, delivered just as well a tempo later. Still better. }) (22. Bd3 { The bishop first. Also fine. } 22... Qd8 23. a5 g6 { Still better: a5 still lands, and his king side pawn move did not help him. }) (22. b5 { The break, but too soon. } 22... Rxc5 23. Rxc5 Rxc5 24. bxa6 bxa6 { The c pawn is gone. Only slightly better. [%csl Rc5] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, remove the guard"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/kVfjx5NJ"]
[FEN "r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ All his pawns are on light squares. One piece guards all his dark squares: the bishop on g7. Remove it. }
1. Nxg7 { The knight takes! Not the bishop: your dark bishop is the attacker and must stay. Queen to f6 is next. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gg5f6][%tail] } (1. Bxg7 { The wrong piece takes. } 1... Nxg7 2. Nf6+ Kf8 { He holds, and your attack is over. Your dark bishop was the attacker. Equal. [%csl Rf6] }) (1. Qh6 { The queen jumps in first, and the bishop simply takes it. } 1... Bxh6 { A whole queen, gone in one move. [%csl Rh6] }) (1. Be5 { Slow: it lets him get his queen to safety before you even touch g7. } 1... Qd8 2. Nxg7 Qxg5 3. fxg5 Nxg7 { The queens are off and he is a pawn up. Worse. }) (1. Nf6+ { The knight gives itself away. } 1... Nxf6 2. Bxf6 Bxf6 3. Qxf6 Re8 { Everything traded, and your queen is alone. Lost. }) 1... Nxg7 { He recaptures the knight — the natural try, but it is the mistake. Stepping the king back to f8 was the only way to survive. [%csl Rg7] } 2. Qf6 2... Kf8 3. Qxg7+ { Check! The queen takes the knight for free — your bishop on b2 guards her down the long diagonal, so the king cannot take back. [%csl Ge7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/t8L5VOxb"]
[FEN "2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1 b - - 0 27"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1_b_-_-_0_27"]

{ New plan: prophylaxis. Ask what he wants, then stop it. He dreams of the back rank, and of h5 and f5. [%csl Re8,Rh5,Rf5][%cal Yg7g6] }
27... g6 { One pawn move: g7 is free for the king, and h5 and f5 are taken from his knight and queen. [%csl Gg7,Gh5,Gf5] } (27... Rcd8 { You double, but the back rank is still thin. } 28. Rfe1 Qc6 29. Nh5 { His knight lands on h5 and hits your bishop. Still a bit better, but he has play. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rdd8 { Back, and the back rank is still a worry. } 28. h4 Qf3 29. Re3 { Only a little better: the rook retreat left the back rank exactly as thin as before. }) (27... Qf3 { The queen jumps in alone. } 28. Re3 Qb7 29. Ne2 { She has to go back. Only a little better. }) (27... h5 { You take h5 with a pawn, and the knight takes the pawn. } 28. Nxh5 Bg5 { A pawn gone. Worse. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rxf4 { Trading the rook for a knight, for no reason. } 28. gxf4 Qf3 29. Qf5 { A rook for a knight, and nothing to show for it. Worse. }) 28. h4 { He grabs space: h5 is his idea. Ask what he wants next, and cover it. [%cal Yb7d7] } 28... Qd7 { Queen to d7: it eyes g4 and h3, and guards the whole back rank. [%cal Gd7g4][%csl Gd7] } (28... h5 { You stop h5 with a pawn, and the knight crashes in. } 29. Nxg6 fxg6 30. Qxg6+ Qg7 { Three pawns for the knight and checks coming. Most of your edge is gone. [%csl Rg6] }) (28... Kf8 { The king goes the wrong way. } 29. h5 Qd7 30. Rc1 { Only a little better: the king walked to the wrong side, and h5 came anyway. }) (28... Rdd8 { The rook goes back, and gives up d4. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. bxc3 { Only a little better: giving up the d file is worth more than the pawn you won back. }) (28... Rd6 { Passive: the rook steps back and does nothing about his back rank ideas. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. Re8+ Rxe8 31. Rxe8+ Kg7 { About equal. The back rank again! [%csl Re8] }) 29. Rfe1 { He doubles rooks. Now the king. [%cal Yg8g7] } 29... Kg7 { The king steps up: no more back rank tricks, ever. Now h5 is your idea, not his. [%csl Gg7][%cal Gh6h5] } (29... Kf8 { The wrong square: the knight crashes in on g6. } 30. Nxg6+ fxg6 31. Qxg6 Qf7 32. Qxh6+ { Three pawns for the knight and your king is bare. Only a little better. [%csl Rg6] }) (29... Qg4 { The queen wanders. } 30. Re4 Qd7 { She comes back. Only a little better. }) (29... Rf8 { A rook move while the king was the point. } 30. h5 g5 31. Ng2 { Equal: the rook move ignored the king, and his king side play came anyway. }) (29... Rxf4 { Rook for knight again. } 30. gxf4 Qg4+ 31. Kf1 Qh3+ { A draw by checks, no more. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, what does he threaten"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/fLvrdyeq"]
[FEN "7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ His rook is on f2 with a discovered check waiting. Before anything else: what does he threaten? [%csl Rf2,Re3] }
1. Kh1 { Step off the diagonal first. Now the discovered check is dead, and your queen is free. [%csl Gh1] } (1. Qa6 { You attack, and he just steps aside. } 1... Kg8 2. h4 Rxc2+ { A pawn gone with check. Equal. [%csl Rc2] }) (1. Qd3 { The queen walks into a discovered check. } 1... Rd2+ 2. Kf1 Rxd3 { A whole queen, lost to a move you saw coming. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. c3 { A pawn move, and his pawn answers. } 1... d5 2. Qxd4 Bxd4 { The queen is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (1. Qxd4 { A free pawn? } 1... Bxd4 { A queen for a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (1. Qc8+ { A check, and a discovered check back. } 1... Rf8+ { Rook to f8 with check, and your queen is lost. [%csl Rc8] }) (1. h3 { Air for the king, but the wrong way. } 1... Rxc2+ { Discovered check, and a pawn. [%csl Rc2] }) 1... h6 { He makes air too. Now your king wants g2, and his bishop wants f4. Stop it. } 2. g3 { One pawn move: f4 is taken from his bishop and g2 is free for your king. [%csl Gg2,Gf4] } (2. h3 { Air, but the bishop comes to f4. } 2... Bf4 3. Qd3 Kg8 { About equal: the bishop got to f4 after all, just one tempo later. [%csl Rf4] }) (2. h4 { The pawn goes too far. } 2... Bf4 3. g3 Bxg3 { A pawn gone. Equal: the h pawn overextended, and his bishop grabbed g3 in return. }) (2. Qc8+ { A check that does nothing. } 2... Kh7 3. g3 Rf1+ { Equal: the check achieved nothing, and his rook is still active on f1. }) (2. Qa6 { The queen hunts pawns. } 2... Kg8 3. Qc8+ Kh7 { Equal: chasing pawns cost time, and he simply tucks the king away safely. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, stop it and push"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/q6pj3WWp"]
[FEN "3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ He wants knight to g6, hitting your queen and rook. Find one move that stops it and pushes your own plan too. }
1... Qf7 { Queen to f7: g6 is covered, and the knight on c4 is attacked. It cannot retreat, or a2 hangs. [%cal Gf7c4,Gf7g6] } (1... Kh7 { The king steps aside, and he trades your good bishop. } 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 3. Ne3 { His knight is safe on e3. Equal. [%csl Ge3] }) (1... Bxc4 { You take first, and the check hurts. } 2. Qxc4+ Qf7 3. Qxf7+ Rxf7 4. Rxd7 { The knight on d7 was hanging. A piece gone. [%csl Rd7] }) (1... Bxe3 { You give up the bishop, and his knight takes back happily. } 2. Nxe3 Qf7 3. Bf3 { His knight is fine and your bishop is gone. Slightly worse. }) (1... b6 { Slow, and Ng6 comes. } 2. Bf3 Qe8 3. b3 { Slightly worse: the knight jump to g6 is still coming, just delayed. }) (1... Rfe8 { The rook moves, and the bishop is traded. } 2. Bf3 b5 3. Bxc5 Qxc5 { Slightly worse: the good bishop is traded off for nothing extra. }) 2. b3 { He guards the knight with a pawn. Take it anyway. } 2... Bxc4 { Bishop takes knight. The pawn must take back, and now c4 is loose. [%cal Gb3c4] } (2... b6 { You wait, and he tidies up. } 3. Bc1 Ne7 4. Bh3 { Equal: waiting let him untangle his pieces for free. }) (2... Bxe3 { The wrong bishop first. } 3. Nxe3 Nb6 4. Bf3 { Equal: this bishop only gave the knight a safe square on e3. }) (2... Ba7 { The bishop steps back. } 3. Bxa7 Nxa7 4. Bf3 { Slightly worse: the bishop trade left the knight on c4 untouched. }) (2... b5 { The pawn hits the knight, and the bishop is traded first. } 3. Bxc5 Nxc5 4. Ne3 { Slightly worse: the knight found a fine new home on c5. }) 3. bxc4 { He takes back. Now the other bishop. } 3... Bxe3 { Bishop takes bishop, and the queen takes back on e3. Then the c4 pawn falls. [%cal Gf7c4] } (3... b6 { Slow: the pawn move does nothing while the bishop on e3 still hangs. } 4. Bc1 Ne7 5. Bh3 { Equal: the extra tempo let him reroute the knight to safety. }) (3... Ba7 { You keep the bishop and lose the pawn on c4. } 4. Bxa7 Nxa7 5. Bf3 { Slightly worse: you keep the bishop pair, but the c4 pawn is gone. }) (3... Ncb8 { The knight goes home. } 4. Bf3 Bxe3 5. Bh5 { Slightly worse: the retreat cost a tempo he did not have to spend. }) 4. Qxe3 { He recaptures with the queen. One more pawn is still there for the taking. } 4... Qxc4 { A pawn up, and his light squares are full of holes. [%csl Gc4] } (4... b6 { You wait, and he guards the pawn. } 5. Qe2 Nc5 6. Nf5 { The pawn is safe now. Only slightly better. [%csl Gc4] }) (4... Ne7 { The knight moves, and c5 comes. } 5. Bh3 Nb6 6. c5 { Equal: the pawn push to c5 gains back just what you won. }) (4... Nb6 { The knight hits c4, and the pawn runs. } 5. c5 Na4 6. Rd5 { Equal: the pawn escapes, and his rook finds an active square too. }) 0-1


