{ He is doubling both rooks on the d column, aimed straight at your queen. Where does she want to go? }
17. Rad1 { Both rooks on the d column, staring at your queen. Find the tidy-up move. [%csl Rd6] } 17... Qc7 { Queen to c7: off the d column, guarding f7 and e5. Nothing is hanging any more. [%csl Gc7][%cal Gc7f7][%tail] } (17... cxd4 { You open up while cramped, and his rooks love it. } 18. Rxd4 Qc7 19. Ne5 { His pieces pour in. Worse. [%csl Rd4] }) (17... Rf8 { A rook move, and the queen still sits on the column. } 18. Ne5 a6 19. Qf4 { Slightly worse: the queen never left the column, so the pressure is still there. }) (17... Qc6 { Off the column, but the f7 pawn is loose. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 19. Qf4 { You went to c7 anyway. Equal. }) (17... Qb8 { Off the column, but too far back. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 19. Qf4 { Back to c7 next move. Equal. }) (17... Qe7 { Off the column, and f7 is covered. Also playable. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 { And you go to c7 anyway. }) (17... Ne4 { The knight jumps, and the queen takes it. } 18. Qxe4 { A whole knight. [%csl Re4] }) 18. h3 18... h6 19. Rd3 19... cxd4 20. Rxd4 20... Re8 { The other rook comes to e8. Both sides have a rook on the open file, nothing hangs anywhere, and the position is dead level. [%csl Ge8] } 0-1
